msgid "Moving from sessions to self-contained requests"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you've been running Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers, you might have bumped into the limitations of stateful sessions.\n"
"Until recently, a client had to perform an `initialize` handshake, get a session ID, and stick to that specific server instance for the duration of the connection."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For a single-instance setup, this is fine.\n"
"But the moment you move to a distributed environment, you're forced into using sticky sessions or a shared session store just to keep the connection alive.\n"
"It's an unnecessary hurdle for what should essentially be a set of HTTP calls."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/[2026-07-28 revision] of the MCP specification finally solves this by going stateless,\n"
"and the 2.0.x line of the https://github.com/quarkiverse/quarkus-mcp-server[Quarkus MCP Server] already supports these changes out of the box.\n"
"Here is a look at how the protocol has evolved in this latest version and how to use it in Quarkus."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The core shift in the July 28 revision is the removal of the stateful session handshake.\n"
"Instead of negotiating a session once, every request is now a self-contained JSON-RPC message."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Everything the server needs — protocol version, client identity, and capabilities — is now packed into a `_meta` field within the request.\n"
"If you're using Streamable HTTP, this metadata is mirrored in headers (`MCP-Protocol-Version`, `Mcp-Method`, and `Mcp-Name`) so the server can route or reject the request before it even parses the body."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Since there is no session state to track, any server instance can handle any request.\n"
"An MCP server is now as easy to scale horizontally as any other stateless REST API."
msgstr ""

msgid "What about versioning?"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Negotiation has also moved to the request level.\n"
"If a server doesn't recognize the version requested in a specific call, it responds with an `UnsupportedProtocolVersionError` and lists its supported versions.\n"
"For clients that want to be proactive, there is now a `server/discover` RPC to check capabilities upfront."
msgstr ""

msgid "Multi Round-Trip Requests"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Going stateless creates one obvious problem though: how does the server ask the client for something?\n"
"Sampling, elicitation, and roots all require the server to reach back out to the client during a request, e.g. to ask the client's LLM a question, collect extra input from the user, or discover what file roots the client exposes.\n"
"Without a persistent session, there is no channel for any of that."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The spec solves this with Multi Round-Trip Requests.\n"
"Instead of pushing a request to the client, the server returns an `input_required` result.\n"
"The client then collects the necessary data and retries the original call with the answers attached.\n"
"Subscriptions follow a similar path: `resources/subscribe` has been replaced by `subscriptions/listen`, where clients receive notifications on a stream based on a specified filter."
msgstr ""

msgid "Implementation in Quarkus MCP Server"
msgstr ""

msgid "Stateless support was added in quarkus-mcp-server 2.0.0 while the spec was still in release candidate."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The implementation is designed to be transparent.\n"
"When a request hits the server, it checks the `MCP-Protocol-Version` header and the `_meta` field.\n"
"If it detects a stateless version (i.e. version `2026-07-28` and up), the request is handled via a transient connection that exists only for the life of that call."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If the request uses an older version, it simply falls back to the traditional session-based path.\n"
"Because both modes live on the same endpoint, you can migrate your clients one by one without breaking existing integrations."
msgstr ""

msgid "Getting started is exactly as it was before:"
msgstr ""

msgid "And your tools remain simple annotated methods:"
msgstr ""

msgid "If you are connecting from a https://github.com/quarkiverse/quarkus-langchain4j[quarkus-langchain4j] application, configure the MCP client with the `streamable-http` transport and point it at your server:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The client auto-detects the protocol version by calling `server/discover` and prefers `2026-07-28` when the server supports it.\n"
"If you want to pin it explicitly — for example to guarantee stateless mode or to force the legacy stateful protocol during a migration — set the `protocol-version` property:"
msgstr ""

msgid "From there, your AI service picks up the tools from that client the same way as before:"
msgstr ""

msgid "No other changes are needed on the client side."
msgstr ""

msgid "Programming for both eras"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For the majority of your tools, you won't even notice the protocol version.\n"
"However, if your tool needs to interact with the client through sampling, elicitation, or roots, you'll need to handle both stateful and stateless flows."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `Sampling`, `Elicitation`, and `Roots` interfaces now provide an `isServerInitiatedRequestSupported()` method, which lets you branch your logic at runtime.\n"
"Here is an example of a tool that asks the client's LLM a question:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the stateful flow, the request is sent across the session and the method blocks until the answer comes back.\n"
"In the stateless flow, the tool throws an `InputRequiredException`, which the extension converts into a `resultType: \"input_required\"` response.\n"
"That way the client knows it needs to send another call once it has the answer.\n"
"For complex multi-step interactions, there is also a `requestState()` which gives you an opaque string the client echoes back on each call so you can track where you left off."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Notifications follow the same pattern: Stateful clients keep using `resources/subscribe`, while stateless clients call `subscriptions/listen` with a filter and receive matching notifications on that stream,\n"
"each tagged with the subscription identifier in `_meta`. List endpoints and `server/discover` responses also now carry `ttlMs` and `cacheScope` fields you can tune through configuration,\n"
"and every JSON-RPC result on the new protocol version includes the server identity in `_meta`, as the spec now requires."
msgstr ""

msgid "MCP Tasks &amp; Extensions"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The new MCP version also includes a way to add capabilities beyond the core MCP protocol, e.g. specific authentication functionality, industry specific logic or experimental features.\n"
"This capability is planned for Quarkus MCP Server 2.1.0, so keep your eyes peeled for that."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"One final note: we run the official https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/conformance[MCP conformance test suite] in CI.\n"
"This ensures the extension isn't just working by its own internal standards, but is strictly adhering to the specification."
msgstr ""

msgid "Getting started"
msgstr "はじめに"

msgid ""
"The 2.0.* release is already available.\n"
"If you're already using quarkus-mcp-server, simply bump your version.\n"
"You can immediately start accepting stateless requests from newer clients without changing a single line of server code."
msgstr ""

msgid "For more details on the Quarkus MCP Server, check out the https://docs.quarkiverse.io/quarkus-mcp-server/dev/[extension documentation]."
msgstr ""
